// Supervision -- keeping actors running. A supervisor starts its children and, when one of them
// crashes, starts it again; if they crash too often, it gives up and crashes itself, so that ITS
// starter learns of it. A supervisor is an ordinary actor, so supervisors nest into trees.
//
// OPT-IN: reach these via `use std::supervisor::*`. Builds on std::actor.
//
// THE LOOP RUNS IN YOUR ACTOR. `supervise(inbox, children, limit)` is a function, not an actor of its
// own: call it from the actor that is to supervise, with that actor's main inbox (a crash is reported
// to the actor that started the child, in its main inbox). The children are built right there, which
// is why they may be of different types -- a `Vec[dyn Supervised]` cannot be sent, and need not be.
//
//     fn workers(inbox: Inbox[()], boss: Pid[Pid[Job]]) -> () {
//       let mut kids: Vec[dyn Supervised] = vec()
//       push(kids, child(actorFn(worker), boss))
//       push(kids, child(actorFn(worker), boss))
//       supervise(inbox, kids, RestartLimit { maxRestarts: 3, withinMs: 5000 })
//     }
//
// THE SIMPLE FORM AND THE FULL ONE. `supervise` is one_for_one with no stop deadline, which is what
// most trees want. `superviseWith(inbox, children, SupervisorSpec { strategy, limit, stopTimeoutMs })`
// is the same loop with the two choices spelled out.
//
// THE THREE STRATEGIES, i.e. what a crash costs:
//   * OneForOne  -- start the crashed child again, and nothing else. The others never notice.
//   * OneForAll  -- the children depend on each other: stop every other one, wait until they have all
//                   ended, then start them all again.
//   * RestForOne -- the children depend on the ones BEFORE them: stop every child started after the
//                   crashed one, wait, then start it and all of them again, in order.
// Waiting means waiting for an end REPORT, which arrives only once that actor's address is free -- so a
// child in a slot is started again where it was, never into a slot its predecessor still holds. More
// than `maxRestarts` restarts within `withinMs` milliseconds, and the supervisor panics, telling every
// child to stop and releasing their addresses first. A group restart counts as ONE restart.
//
// A CHILD WITH A FIXED ADDRESS. A child made with `child(a, init)` gets a new address at every
// restart. `childIn(slot, a, init)` puts it in a `Slot` instead (std::actor): the address then stays
// the same across restarts, and a send while the child is being started again waits in the slot
// instead of being dropped. Make the slot where the address is needed and hand out `slot.pid()`.
//
// A CHILD THAT NEVER RECEIVES CANNOT BE STOPPED, and this is the one thing to design for. `stop` is a
// message; an actor whose loop is its own work never reads it. Such an actor must ask
// `inbox.stopRequested()` in its loop (std::actor) -- then it is stoppable like any other. For the one
// that does not, `stopTimeoutMs` decides what the supervisor does rather than waiting forever:
//   * 0 (the default) -- wait for the reports. The supervisor stays receive-ready throughout, so it
//     still answers Stop and the program still ends; only that group stands still.
//   * more -- give up after that many milliseconds. On a RESTART the supervisor panics, naming the
//     child, so its own starter learns of it: it must not go on pretending the group is intact. On
//     SHUTDOWN it abandons that child, releases its address and returns normally -- its job there is to
//     end, and a crash would only report a failure to a parent that is itself shutting down.
//
// SHUTDOWN. At `Stop` the supervisor stops its children in REVERSE start order -- the order a child
// that depends on an earlier one needs -- and releases every address. With a deadline it waits for each
// child to end before it stops the next; without one it tells them all and returns, because after Stop
// every receive answers Stop at once and a supervisor can no longer wait on its inbox.
//
// LIMITS (deliberate):
//   * A child is started again only after a CRASH. A child that returns stays ended.
//   * A child made with `child` has a NEW address after a restart, so whoever must reach it either
//     lives on `childIn`, or learns the address from the child itself when it starts.
//   * The main program cannot supervise: it never receives Stop, so the program would never end.
use std::actor::*
use std::time::*

// A child a supervisor can start again after it crashed: its function and its start value.
pub struct Child[M, I] { run: ActorFn[M, I], init: I }

// The child that runs `run` with the start value `init`.
pub fn child[M, I](run: ActorFn[M, I], init: I) -> Child[M, I] { Child { run: run, init: init } }

// A child that lives at a fixed address: every actor started for it receives on the same slot.
pub struct SlotChild[M, I] { at: Slot[M], run: ActorFn[M, I], init: I }

// The child that runs `run` with the start value `init`, always at the address `at`.
pub fn childIn[M, I](at: Slot[M], run: ActorFn[M, I], init: I) -> SlotChild[M, I] {
  SlotChild { at: at, run: run, init: init }
}

// Anything a supervisor can start, whatever its message type: what a `Vec[dyn Supervised]` holds.
pub trait Supervised {
  // Start it once more and return the id its crash report will carry.
  fn start(self) -> ActorId

  // Give up its address, if it has one of its own. A supervisor does this when it stops supervising.
  fn release(self) -> ()
}

impl[M, I] Supervised for Child[M, I] {
  fn start(self) -> ActorId { self.run.spawn(self.init).actorId() }
  fn release(self) -> () {}
}

impl[M, I] Supervised for SlotChild[M, I] {
  fn start(self) -> ActorId { self.at.spawn(self.run, self.init) }
  fn release(self) -> () { self.at.release() }
}

// Give up when more than `maxRestarts` restarts happen within `withinMs` milliseconds.
pub struct RestartLimit { maxRestarts: Int, withinMs: Int }

// What a crash costs the SIBLINGS of the child that crashed. See the top of this module.
pub enum Strategy { OneForOne, OneForAll, RestForOne }

// Everything a supervisor decides: how a crash spreads, how often it may happen, and how long a child
// may take to stop (0 = as long as it takes).
pub struct SupervisorSpec { strategy: Strategy, limit: RestartLimit, stopTimeoutMs: Int }

// Start every child, start again each one that crashes, and return at Stop (see the top of this
// module). The simple form: one_for_one, and a stop takes as long as it takes.
pub fn supervise[M](inbox: Inbox[M], children: Vec[dyn Supervised], limit: RestartLimit) -> () {
  superviseWith(inbox, children, SupervisorSpec {
    strategy: Strategy::OneForOne,
    limit: limit,
    stopTimeoutMs: 0
  })
}

// The full form, with the strategy and the stop deadline spelled out.
pub fn superviseWith[M](inbox: Inbox[M], children: Vec[dyn Supervised], spec: SupervisorSpec) -> () {
  if spec.limit.maxRestarts < 0 { panic("supervise: maxRestarts must not be negative") }
  if spec.limit.withinMs < 1 { panic("supervise: withinMs must be at least 1") }
  if spec.stopTimeoutMs < 0 {
    panic("supervise: stopTimeoutMs must not be negative (0 = wait as long as it takes)")
  }
  let clock = startStopwatch()
  let mut running: Vec[ActorId] = vec()
  let mut state: Vec[Int] = vec()
  for c in children {
    push(running, c.start())
    push(state, _UP)
  }
  // When the recent restarts happened, in milliseconds since the start, oldest first.
  let mut recent: Vec[Int] = vec()
  // While a group is stopping: the first child to start again once every one of them has ended.
  let mut restartFrom = -1
  let mut deadline = 0
  loop {
    let mail = if _countStopping(state) > 0 && spec.stopTimeoutMs > 0 {
      let left = deadline - clock.elapsedMillis()
      if left <= 0 { None } else { inbox.receiveTimeout(left) }
    } else {
      Some(inbox.receive())
    }
    match mail {
      // The deadline passed and a child we told to stop has still not ended. Give the addresses up --
      // one left behind would take messages nobody reads -- and let our own starter hear of it.
      None => {
        for c in children { c.release() }
        panic("supervisor: ${_stillStopping(state)} did not stop within ${spec.stopTimeoutMs} ms" +
              " (an actor that never receives must ask inbox.stopRequested())")
      },
      Some(Mail::Msg(_)) => {},
      Some(Mail::Stop) => {
        _shutdown(inbox, children, running, state, clock, spec.stopTimeoutMs)
        break
      },
      Some(Mail::Exited(id, why)) => {
        let i = _indexOf(running, id)
        if i >= 0 {
          if state[i] == _STOPPING {
            // A child we asked to stop has ended. When the last one has, the group starts again.
            state[i] = _DOWN
            if _countStopping(state) == 0 && restartFrom >= 0 {
              _startDown(children, running, state, restartFrom)
              restartFrom = -1
            }
          } else if state[i] == _UP {
            // A crash. (A second report for a child already down -- a child that crashed while it was
            // stopping sends one of each -- falls through both arms and is ignored.)
            state[i] = _DOWN
            let now = clock.elapsedMillis()
            recent = _since(recent, now - spec.limit.withinMs)
            if len(recent) >= spec.limit.maxRestarts {
              _tellToStop(running, state)
              for c in children { c.release() }
              panic("supervisor: more than ${spec.limit.maxRestarts} restarts within" +
                    " ${spec.limit.withinMs} ms; the last: ${why}")
            }
            push(recent, now)
            match spec.strategy {
              Strategy::OneForOne => {
                running[i] = children[i].start()
                state[i] = _UP
              },
              Strategy::OneForAll => {
                _stopGroup(inbox, running, state, 0, i)
                restartFrom = 0
              },
              Strategy::RestForOne => {
                _stopGroup(inbox, running, state, i + 1, -1)
                restartFrom = i
              },
            }
            if restartFrom >= 0 {
              if _countStopping(state) > 0 {
                deadline = clock.elapsedMillis() + spec.stopTimeoutMs
              } else {
                // Every sibling was down already: there is nothing to wait for.
                _startDown(children, running, state, restartFrom)
                restartFrom = -1
              }
            }
          }
        }
      },
    }
  }
}

// The three states a child is in, as seen from here.
const _DOWN: Int = 0
const _UP: Int = 1
const _STOPPING: Int = 2

// Tell every child from `from` on that is up to stop, and watch it first, so exactly one end report
// arrives for each -- also for one that has just ended by itself ("gone", at once). `except` is the
// child that is already down (the crashed one) and is not to be told; -1 means none.
fn _stopGroup[M](inbox: Inbox[M], running: Vec[ActorId], mut state: Vec[Int], from: Int, except: Int) -> () {
  let mut i = from
  while i < len(state) {
    if i != except && state[i] == _UP {
      let _watching = running[i].watch(inbox)
      let _told = running[i].stop()
      state[i] = _STOPPING
    }
    i += 1
  }
}

// Tell every child that is up to stop, without watching: for the path that panics right afterwards.
fn _tellToStop(running: Vec[ActorId], state: Vec[Int]) -> () {
  let mut i = 0
  while i < len(state) {
    if state[i] == _UP { let _told = running[i].stop() }
    i += 1
  }
}

// Start every child from `from` on that is down, in order.
fn _startDown(children: Vec[dyn Supervised], mut running: Vec[ActorId], mut state: Vec[Int], from: Int) -> () {
  let mut i = from
  while i < len(state) {
    if state[i] == _DOWN {
      running[i] = children[i].start()
      state[i] = _UP
    }
    i += 1
  }
}

// How many children we are waiting for.
fn _countStopping(state: Vec[Int]) -> Int {
  let mut n = 0
  for s in state { if s == _STOPPING { n += 1 } }
  n
}

// Which ones, for the message of a deadline that passed.
fn _stillStopping(state: Vec[Int]) -> String {
  let mut out = ""
  let mut i = 0
  while i < len(state) {
    if state[i] == _STOPPING {
      out = if out == "" { "child ${i}" } else { "${out}, ${i}" }
    }
    i += 1
  }
  out
}

// Stop the children in reverse start order and release every address. With a deadline, each child is
// waited for before the next is told, so one that depends on an earlier one still has it. The wait is
// POLLED, not blocked on: after Stop every receive answers Stop at once, so the inbox can no longer
// hold this actor. Without a deadline the children are told and we return -- there is nothing to wait
// on, and the world is joining its threads.
fn _shutdown[M](inbox: Inbox[M], children: Vec[dyn Supervised], running: Vec[ActorId],
                mut state: Vec[Int], clock: Stopwatch, timeoutMs: Int) -> () {
  let mut i = len(state) - 1
  while i >= 0 {
    if state[i] == _UP {
      let _watching = running[i].watch(inbox)
      let _told = running[i].stop()
      state[i] = _STOPPING
      let deadline = clock.elapsedMillis() + timeoutMs
      let mut waiting = timeoutMs > 0
      while waiting {
        _drain(inbox, running, state)
        if state[i] != _STOPPING {
          waiting = false
        } else if clock.elapsedMillis() >= deadline {
          waiting = false            // abandoned: its address is released below like every other
        } else {
          sleep(1)
        }
      }
    }
    i -= 1
  }
  for c in children { c.release() }
}

// Take what is already in the inbox, without waiting: the end reports of children we told to stop.
fn _drain[M](inbox: Inbox[M], running: Vec[ActorId], mut state: Vec[Int]) -> () {
  let mut more = true
  while more {
    match inbox.receiveTimeout(0) {
      Some(Mail::Exited(id, _)) => {
        let i = _indexOf(running, id)
        if i >= 0 && state[i] == _STOPPING { state[i] = _DOWN }
      },
      Some(Mail::Msg(_)) => {},
      Some(Mail::Stop) => { more = false },
      None => { more = false },
    }
  }
}

// The position of `id` in `ids`, or -1: a report from an actor that is not one of the children.
fn _indexOf(ids: Vec[ActorId], id: ActorId) -> Int {
  let mut i = 0
  let mut found = -1
  while found < 0 && i < len(ids) {
    if ids[i] == id { found = i }
    i += 1
  }
  found
}

// The times after `cutoff`.
fn _since(times: Vec[Int], cutoff: Int) -> Vec[Int] {
  let mut kept: Vec[Int] = vec()
  for t in times { if t > cutoff { push(kept, t) } }
  kept
}
